Capsule deprecation: spec.limitRanges -> LimitRange objects rendered directly
=============================================================================
Date : 2026-08-02
Mode : LIVE on kind `kind-platform-agent`, Capsule chart 0.13.10, v1beta2
Gate : make check 1607 -> 1608 (+1)

Plan item : NEXT_PLAN 결정 3 ("Capsule limitRanges 이관 경로")
Decision  : DECISIONS.md D41 — option C (render the object), not either replacement
Brief     : docs/plans/2026-08-02-capsule-limitranges-path.md


The question was posed with two answers, and the third was already in the repo
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Four documents recorded the same fork: `GlobalTenantResource` (cluster-scoped ->
violates D30) vs `TenantResource` (needs a ServiceAccount + RBAC so Capsule can
replicate on our behalf). Both cost something, hence "a decision, not a commit".

But `docs/evidence/capsule-deprecation-metadata.log:26` had already written the
third answer down, for the sibling field deprecated in the same release:

    `networkPolicies` does not apply to us — this repo renders NetworkPolicy
    objects directly rather than through the Tenant spec.

`limitRanges` takes the same move. A `LimitRange` is namespaced, so nothing
cluster-scoped is created and D30 is untouched; and no new permission surface is
needed, because there is no deputy to authorise — the identity that already
creates this namespace's ServiceAccount, Role, RoleBinding and NetworkPolicy can
create a LimitRange. `TenantResource` needs RBAC precisely because Capsule, not
us, would be doing the writing.

What is given up is Capsule's reconciliation. That is the same trade already
accepted for the NetworkPolicies, and both sets derive from `namespaces_for`, so
a tenant namespace cannot appear without one.


1. Is the field load-bearing, or is this cosmetic?
----------------------------------------------------
Load-bearing, and the failure is silent where it matters.

  BEFORE (LimitRange present), pod with `requests` only:
    $ kubectl apply -f nolimits-pod.yaml       # ns acme-dev-logging
    pod/lr-probe-nolimits created
    $ kubectl get pod ... -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].resources}'
    {"limits":{"cpu":"500m","memory":"512Mi"},
     "requests":{"cpu":"10m","memory":"32Mi"}}      <- default injected

  The quota renders as `limits.cpu`/`limits.memory` (tenancy.py), and Kubernetes
  turns a `limits.*` quota into an admission requirement on every container.

  $ kubectl get resourcequota -n acme-dev-logging
    capsule-acme-dev-0  map[limits.cpu:16 limits.memory:64Gi pods:199]

  None of the four tenant add-on values files (kube-prometheus-stack, loki,
  tempo, argo-rollouts) sets limits — the only `limits:` under
  infra/onprem/addons/values/ belongs to capsule itself, which runs in
  capsule-system. So every tenant workload depends on these defaults. So does
  `verify_tenant_isolation.py`, whose probe pods set requests only.

  Where it would surface: a chart whose pods are refused at admission still
  leaves the Argo Application reading Synced, with zero pods (Open Risk 8).


2. Does Capsule reclaim its own LimitRange when the field is removed?
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Yes — asked the API server instead of reading the docs.

  $ kubectl patch tenant acme-dev --type=json \
        -p='[{"op":"remove","path":"/spec/limitRanges"}]'
  tenant.capsule.clastix.io/acme-dev patched

  ~8s later, all four acme LimitRanges are gone; globex (still declaring the
  field at that point) keeps its own:

    NAMESPACE                  NAME
    globex-dev-observability   capsule-globex-dev-0

  So the two mechanisms never overlap: Capsule cleans up as we take over.

  And with them gone, the admission requirement bites for real:

    $ kubectl apply -f nolimits-pod.yaml
    Error from server (Forbidden): pods "lr-probe-nolimits" is forbidden:
    failed quota: capsule-acme-dev-0: must specify limits.cpu for: c;
    limits.memory for: c

  This is the state the removal release would have left us in, silently.


3. Does the directly-rendered LimitRange work, and does Capsule leave it alone?
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  $ kubectl apply -f - < render_tenancy.py acme -e dev
    limitrange/acme-defaults created        (x4, one per tenant namespace)
  $ kubectl apply -f nolimits-pod.yaml
    pod/lr-probe-nolimits created
  $ ... -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[0].resources.limits}'
    {"cpu":"500m","memory":"512Mi"}          <- admitted again

  Capsule's webhook stamps its own labels onto our object:

    capsule.clastix.io/managed-by=acme-dev, projectcapsule.dev/tenant=acme-dev

  That label is why "it survived for a minute" was not accepted as an answer —
  an object Capsule believes it manages is an object Capsule might prune. The
  strongest available test is a full resync, so the controller was restarted:

    $ kubectl rollout restart deploy/capsule-controller-manager -n capsule-system
    deployment "capsule-controller-manager" successfully rolled out

    t+15s .. t+120s : LimitRange count = 5, probe pod Running   (8 samples)

  All five survive (4 acme + 1 globex), across a controller that started from
  scratch with no Tenant declaring `limitRanges`.


4. Side effect: the deprecation warnings are now gone entirely
----------------------------------------------------------------
    $ kubectl apply -f <rendered acme/dev>  2>apply.err
    $ wc -c < apply.err
    0

  Both deprecated fields have now left the Tenant spec (`additionalMetadata` on
  2026-07-28, `limitRanges` here), so applying a tenant writes nothing to stderr.

  This retires the *example* behind `test_tenancy_tools.py::CAPSULE_WARNINGS` but
  not the behaviour it guards — kubectl warns from many sources, and any of them
  re-creates the original bug (warning-only stderr read as a failed step). The
  fixture is now labelled HISTORICAL rather than quietly refreshed to something
  nobody observed; the guard against these two fields returning lives in
  test_tenancy.py, where it asserts the renderer instead of a string.


Guards added (both falsified individually)
--------------------------------------------
  test_the_deprecated_capsule_field_is_gone
      remove-and-restore: putting `limitRanges` back ->
      "AssertionError: the deprecated field is back"

  test_a_limits_quota_is_never_rendered_without_the_defaults_that_satisfy_it
      DERIVED, not enumerated: for every tenant/env whose rendered quota names a
      `limits.` resource, every rendered Namespace must have a LimitRange with
      container defaults. Dropping the emit ->
      "acme/dev: quota declares ['limits.cpu','limits.memory','pods'] but
       ['acme-dev-logging',...] get no container defaults"
      A namespace added later without defaults fails the same way — which is the
      part an enumerated guard would have missed (the D36/D37 lesson).


Reproduce
-----------
  kubectl apply -f - <<< "$(python scripts/render_tenancy.py acme -e dev)"
  kubectl get limitrange -A --show-labels
  kubectl get tenant acme-dev -o jsonpath='{.spec.limitRanges}'   # empty
